    "MEDICATIONS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Antiviral drugs have not been tested for efficacy.",
          "pdf_page": 855,
          "printed_page": 773,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Antibiotics to control secondary bacterial infections recommended if fever, lethargy, or inappetence accompany mucopurulent nasal discharge—amoxicillin, amoxicillin– clavulanic acid, and doxycycline are empiric first-line options for mild cases.",
          "pdf_page": 855,
          "printed_page": 773,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Clinical pneumonia—first-line recommendations include parenteral administration of fluoroquinolone and penicillin (ampicillin) or clindamycin initially until antimicrobial culture and sensitivity available.",
          "pdf_page": 855,
          "printed_page": 773,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "text": "Resistant bacteria—important to culture and",
          "pdf_page": 855,
          "printed_page": 773,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "establish bacterial sensitivity; consider inherent resistance of Mycoplasma to penicillins and cephalosporins; for organisms resistant to first-choice antibiotic therapies above, consider aminoglycosides (gentamicin or amikacin, contraindicated in animals with renal dysfunction, dehydration), cephalosporins (cephalexin, cefazolin, cefadroxil, cefoxitin, cefovecin); see Suggested Reading.",
          "pdf_page": 855,
          "printed_page": 773,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "text": "In absence of controlled studies on",
          "pdf_page": 855,
          "printed_page": 773,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "efficacy, inhalational antimicrobial therapy not recommended.",
          "pdf_page": 855,
          "printed_page": 773,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "In animals treated for pneumonia—recheck patient and radiographs after 10–14 days to decide whether continued antibiotic therapy indicated; optimal duration of therapy for pneumonia unknown; shorter courses of antibiotics (as used in humans with pneumonia) than traditionally recommended 4–6 weeks may be effective.",
          "pdf_page": 855,
          "printed_page": 773,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Cough suppressants (butorphanol or hydrocodone bitartrate)—often effective in suppressing dry nonproductive cough; cough suppression counterproductive in animals with pneumonia.",
          "pdf_page": 855,
          "printed_page": 773,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Bronchodilators (theophylline or amino phylline)—may relieve wheezing.",
          "pdf_page": 855,
          "printed_page": 773,
          "column": 3
        }
      ],
